[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Electrical Requirements
UID: DOC-ELECTRICAL

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: hardware.sgra

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Electrical requirements. Each one Refines a ``SYS-`` requirement.

``ELE-001``, ``ELE-002``, ``ELE-003`` and ``ELE-004`` carry the electrical
share of the ``SYS-004`` loss budget: 25 W plus 40 W plus 100 W plus 15 W is
180 W, and ``MEC-001`` carries the remaining 120 W, summing to the parent's
300 W.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-001
TITLE: TX inverter loss
STATEMENT: >>>
The transmitter H-bridge, including its gate drive, shall dissipate no more than
25 W at 3.0 kW output.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
At 3.0 kW into a 400 V bus the series-resonant tank carries 8.33 A rms, so each
switch conducts about 5.89 A rms. With 30 milliohm devices that is 4.2 W of
conduction across the four switches, leaving about 21 W for switching loss and
gate drive at 85 kHz - achievable with a modern GaN part and a tight commutation
loop. The v1 LMG2610 would spend 23.6 W on conduction alone and leave 1.4 W,
which is why it cannot meet this number. See ``docs/design/v1-baseline.md``
finding F1.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
BUDGET: 25 W
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-004
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-002
TITLE: RX rectifier loss
STATEMENT: >>>
The receiver rectifier, including its output filter, shall dissipate no more
than 40 W at 3.0 kW throughput.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
At about 7.5 A of rectified current, a two-diode conduction path costs
2 times Vf times 7.5 A: 25.5 W with the v1 silicon ultrafast parts at 1.7 V,
22.5 W with a 650 V SiC Schottky at 1.5 V, and 3.4 W with synchronous
rectification using 30 milliohm devices. Only the last leaves real margin for
switching loss at 100 kHz, so this budget is deliberately tight enough to force
that trade to be made explicitly rather than inherited from v1.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
BUDGET: 40 W
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-004
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-003
TITLE: Vehicle-side converter loss
STATEMENT: >>>
The vehicle-side dual active bridge, from rectified DC to pack terminals, shall
dissipate no more than 100 W at 3.0 kW throughput.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
CORRECTED 2026-08-31 against simulation. The earlier figures in this field -
74 W for two devices per branch and 37 W for four - were understated about
twofold, because the half-cycle conduction factor was dropped. Each switch
position conducts for half the cycle, so a device carries I rms over root two
times N with N in parallel, and the whole low-voltage bridge costs
2 times I rms squared times R ds over N.

``sim/dab/run_dab.py`` puts 70.7 A rms in the tank at 3.0 kW and the most
favourable phase shift. That gives 152 W for the v1 choice of two
BSC190N15NS3 per branch, and 76 W for four. The conclusion the old numbers
drove - four devices per branch rather than two - is unchanged and
strengthened, because two per branch now exceeds this entire requirement by
52% on conduction alone.

The 100 W budget itself is NOT met and this requirement is not close to
satisfied. Four per branch spends 76 W of the 100 W on low-voltage conduction
before the high-voltage bridge, the transformer or any switching loss. Either
the allocation rises, the device changes, or the phase shift and frequency move.
That is E1's to settle and it is why this requirement stays Draft.

R ds(on) here is the 19 milliohm family figure for BSC190N15NS3 scaled 1.6 times
for temperature and is NOT confirmed against a fetched datasheet - recorded as
blocked in ``docs/reference/manifest.yaml``. The shortfall is real at any
plausible value; its size is not yet pinned. See
``docs/design/sim-findings.md`` finding F17.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
EVIDENCE: >>>
``docs/design/sim-dab.md``, run 2026-08-31 on ngspice-42, cross-checked against
the single-phase-shift closed form to within 1.6% on every point, corners swept
over phase shift 30 to 90 degrees and 100 to 150 kHz. Low-voltage bridge
conduction is 76 W of the 100 W allowed, at four devices per branch and the most
favourable corner, with nothing yet allocated to the high-voltage bridge, the
transformer or switching loss. NOT SATISFIED - this evidence is what shows the
budget does not close.
<<<
BUDGET: 100 W
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-004
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: sim/dab/dab.cir

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-004
TITLE: Auxiliary and control loss
STATEMENT: >>>
All auxiliary rails, isolated bias supplies, sensing and digital control across
all boards shall together dissipate no more than 15 W.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Three STM32 nodes, four isolated gate-drive bias supplies, digital isolators and
the sensing chain. 15 W is generous for that population and exists mainly so the
auxiliary tree cannot quietly grow into the power budget; it also bounds the
auxiliary converter sizing, which the v1 white paper never stated consistently.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Analysis
BUDGET: 15 W
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-004
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-005
TITLE: TX switch current rating
STATEMENT: >>>
Each transmitter bridge switch shall be rated for a continuous drain current of
at least 17 A and a repetitive peak of at least 24 A at its operating junction
temperature.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The tank carries 8.33 A rms and 11.8 A peak at 3.0 kW. A factor of two on both
figures covers the transient during resonance re-acquisition, coupling steps at
docking, and the derating of any device rating quoted at 25 degC. The v1 part
was carried on a 6.4 A number that is in fact a low-side absolute-maximum peak,
with the high-side limit of 4 A not mentioned at all; this requirement is
written in continuous terms specifically so that a peak rating cannot be quoted
against it.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Analysis
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-001
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-006
TITLE: TX switch voltage derating
STATEMENT: >>>
Every switch on the 400 V bus shall have a drain-source rating of at least 650 V
and shall not exceed 80% of that rating including all ringing and transient
overshoot.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
400 V on a 600 V part is a factor of 1.5, which leaves nothing for the ringing a
fast GaN edge produces across an imperfect commutation loop. 650 V at 80% gives
a 520 V ceiling and 120 V of headroom over the bus. The v1 HV bridge used a
600 V CoolMOS and the v1 gate driver was quoted at 650 V when the real part is
rated 600 V; both are inside this requirement's reach.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-001
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-007
TITLE: Resonant tank capacitor rating
STATEMENT: >>>
The series compensation capacitor on each side shall be rated for at least
1500 V dc, at least 900 V rms at the operating frequency, and a continuous
ripple current of at least 12 A rms at 85 kHz.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
At the matched operating point the tuning capacitor is about 22 nF standing off
720 V rms and carrying the full 8.33 A of tank current. The ratings above apply
a factor of about 1.25 on voltage and 1.4 on current. This part is likely the
largest passive in the system and it appears nowhere in the v1 white paper; the
requirement exists so it is specified rather than discovered. See
``docs/design/v1-baseline.md`` finding F10.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Analysis
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-001
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-008
TITLE: Low-voltage bridge current capability
STATEMENT: >>>
The vehicle-side low-voltage bridge and its interconnect shall carry 62.5 A
continuous at 48 V without any conductor exceeding a 30 degC rise above the
local ambient.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
3.0 kW at 48 V is 62.5 A, and at the bottom of the pack window in ``SYS-003``
it is 75 A. 30 degC is the conventional PCB copper rise limit that keeps
laminate well inside its rating and keeps the copper resistance from running
away with temperature.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-001
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-009
TITLE: Hardware over-current trip
STATEMENT: >>>
Each bridge shall have an over-current comparator wired directly to the timer
break input of its controller, latching all outputs off within 10 microseconds
of the threshold being crossed, without firmware participation.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Software cannot save a bridge from a shoot-through. The v1 firmware disables the
break input on every timer it configures and has no hardware trip anywhere, so
the only protection is the internal cycle-by-cycle limit of a part that is
itself being operated far outside its ratings. 10 microseconds is roughly one
switching period at 85 kHz, which is the longest a fault may persist without
damage.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-008
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-010
TITLE: Tank current sense bandwidth and accuracy
STATEMENT: >>>
The tank current sense chain shall have a small-signal bandwidth of at least
1 MHz and shall resolve the tank current to better than 2% of full scale over
the 80 kHz to 120 kHz band.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Resonance tracking hill-climbs on this signal, so its noise floor sets how
finely the algorithm can discriminate between adjacent frequency points. 1 MHz
is roughly the tenth harmonic at the top of the band, enough to reproduce the
waveform rather than a smeared version of it. 2% is what makes a hill-climb step
of a few hundred hertz detectable above the measurement itself. The v1 white
paper reports this chain as the dominant noise source in the control loop.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-009
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-011
TITLE: Isolation between dock and vehicle domains
STATEMENT: >>>
Every signal and bias path crossing between the high-voltage bus domain and the
low-voltage control domain shall be galvanically isolated to a working voltage
of at least 800 V rms, reinforced.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
800 V rms working covers the 400 V bus with a factor of two, and reinforced
rather than basic isolation is what allows a single insulation failure not to
energise the control domain. The v1 design already uses an appropriate isolation
set; this requirement records the number those parts must be selected against.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-005
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-012
TITLE: Output voltage regulation and ripple
STATEMENT: >>>
In constant-voltage mode the output shall hold its setpoint within 1% across the
full load range, with peak-to-peak ripple at the pack terminals below 1% of the
setpoint.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
1% regulation is finer than the top-of-charge tolerance of any lithium chemistry
and so does not itself constrain the pack; it is set here so the constant-voltage
taper is governed by the charge profile rather than by converter droop. The
ripple limit bounds the ripple current the pack sees, which is what shortens
cell life at the top of charge.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-003
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-013
TITLE: Board fabrication capability class
STATEMENT: >>>
No board shall require a track or gap below 100 micrometres, a drill below
0.2 mm, a blind or buried via, or more than 6 layers.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the capability class every volume prototype house quotes as standard, so
staying inside it keeps the boards orderable anywhere and keeps the cost step
that comes with sequential lamination out of the programme. The high-current
layers of the low-voltage bridge are the constraint that makes 2 oz copper part
of the class rather than an option.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-011
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-014
TITLE: Electrical BOM traceability
STATEMENT: >>>
Every electrical component in a released BOM shall have an entry in
``docs/reference/manifest.yaml`` naming the datasheet revision its ratings were
read from and the date it was retrieved.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The v1 review found the transmitter power stage carried on an absolute-maximum
peak quoted as a continuous rating, a gate driver quoted 50 V above its actual
rating, and six part numbers that do not name orderable parts. Recording the
revision is what catches a datasheet that changed under the design.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-010
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-015
TITLE: In-band modulator and demodulator
STATEMENT: >>>
Each end shall be able to impress a detectable modulation on the tank and to
demodulate the other end's, recovering a symbol at a bit error rate below 1e-4
across the full range of coupling and throughput of ``SYS-006`` and
``SYS-012``.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
1e-4 is what lets a short frame with a CRC succeed on the first attempt almost
always, so the link does not spend its limited no-load bandwidth on retries. The
demodulator has to work across a 50-fold swing in tank bandwidth and a
corresponding swing in received amplitude, which is the real difficulty here -
not the noise floor. Load-shift keying from the receiver and frequency-shift
keying from the transmitter is the conventional pairing and is the starting
assumption.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-012
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-016
TITLE: Handshake damping network
STATEMENT: >>>
The transmitter shall be able to switch a damping network across its tank that
lowers the loaded quality factor to 5 or below, dissipating no more than 20 W at
the handshake drive level, and shall not engage it above the handshake drive
level.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The no-load tank gives only 867 Hz of bandwidth, which is the binding constraint
on ``SYS-012``. A 22 ohm shunt brings the loaded Q to about 3.8 and the
bandwidth to 22.5 kHz - a 26-fold improvement - and at a 20 V fundamental drive
it burns 18 W, which is affordable for the duration of a handshake. The
prohibition above handshake level is a safety interlock: the same network across
the tank at full drive would dissipate kilowatts.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-012
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-017
TITLE: Handshake drive level
STATEMENT: >>>
The transmitter shall be able to drive the tank at a reduced amplitude of 20 V
fundamental or less, and shall deliver no more than 50 W into a short-circuited
or absent receiver at that level.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The level at which ``SYS-013`` permits the dock to probe before a receiver is
identified. 50 W into an unknown load is low enough not to heat a foreign object
dangerously or damage a flooded receiver, while being enough for the receiver
electronics to wake on harvested energy and answer.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-013
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ELE-018
TITLE: Low-voltage bridge is synchronous in both directions
STATEMENT: >>>
The low-voltage bridge shall be a fully synchronous bridge capable of both
rectification and inversion at the full 75 A of ``ELE-008``, with no reliance on
body-diode conduction in steady state in either direction.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
``SYS-015`` deploys one converter design in both orientations, so the
low-voltage bridge rectifies in the Mako instance and inverts in the Leviathan
instance. It must therefore be actively driven in both, which a passive
rectifier could not be. This is not only a cost: at 62.5 A a passive
low-voltage rectifier would spend tens of watts in diode drops that a
synchronous bridge does not, so the bidirectional requirement and the
``ELE-003`` loss budget push the same way.

The body-diode clause matters because these devices' body diodes have poor
reverse recovery relative to the channel, and steady-state body-diode conduction
at 75 A would dominate the loss budget while producing exactly the recovery
behaviour a dual active bridge is meant to avoid.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-015
  ROLE: Refines
